.TH HU_POLL 3 "" "helios-unix" "Library functions"
.SH NAME
hu_poll \- wait for readiness on supported Helios descriptors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <helios/unix/poll.h>
struct hu_pollfd { int fd; short events, revents; };
int hu_poll(struct hu_pollfd *fds, unsigned count, int timeout_ms);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Wait on /internet sockets with readiness support, native FIFO channels, or
regular files. Regular files report immediate read/write readiness.
Terminals and native anonymous pipes are not supported: the call fails with
EOPNOTSUPP. This is a restricted interface, not a general POSIX poll replacement.
.PP
Request HU_POLLIN, HU_POLLOUT and/or HU_POLLPRI in events. Inspect revents after
every successful call. HU_POLLERR, HU_POLLHUP and HU_POLLNVAL are reported even
when not requested. Invalid nonnegative descriptors get HU_POLLNVAL; negative
entries are ignored. Duplicate entries count separately. Buffered data can
coexist with HU_POLLHUP; drain the input before treating it as EOF.
.PP
timeout_ms is in milliseconds: 0 tests immediately, any negative value waits
indefinitely. Readiness is a hint to retry I/O, not a guarantee that a whole
buffer can be read or written. Use nonblocking I/O for an event loop.
The implementation uses native readiness notifications rather than repeated
network polling and preserves the original deadline across spurious wakes.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
#include <helios/unix/poll.h>

/* Return readiness bits, zero on timeout, or -1 with errno set. */
int wait_for_input(int fd)
{
    struct hu_pollfd p;
    int result;
    p.fd = fd;
    p.events = HU_POLLIN;
    p.revents = 0;
    result = hu_poll(&p, 1, 5000);
    return result > 0 ? p.revents : result;
}
.EE
.SH RETURN VALUE
Number of entries with nonzero revents, 0 for timeout, or -1 with errno set.
EINTR means the wait was interrupted. EINVAL indicates invalid arguments,
including count above 65536. fds may be NULL when count is zero.
.SH SEE ALSO
hu_channel(3), hu_set_nonblock(3), hu_tls_fd(3)
